<BaseLayout title="Methodology" description="APT Notes methodology for scope, sourcing, naming, attribution, confidence and corrections."><article class="shell narrow prose"><p class="eyebrow">Editorial standards</p><h1>Methodology</h1><h2>Scope</h2><p>APT Notes is a curated dossier collection, not an exhaustive threat-actor directory. It covers publicly documented actors and only the operations, entities and behaviours needed to understand the selected profiles. Inclusion requires a durable research purpose and sufficient public sourcing.</p><h2>Inclusion criteria</h2><p>Profiles require a stable identifier, meaningful summary, review date, confidence statement and normalized sources. Drafts and placeholders are never published.</p><h2>Review freshness</h2><p>Published actor profiles are reviewed at least annually. Records up to 180 days old are current, records between 181 and 365 days are due for review, and older records are marked stale. Review freshness is calculated from the profile's public <code>last_reviewed</code> date and does not imply that the actor became inactive.</p><h2>Naming and aliases</h2><p class="callout">Threat actor names are analytical constructs. Different organisations may use different names for overlapping, broader or narrower sets of activity.</p><p>Aliases are source-mapped and assigned a relationship type. A vendor tracking cluster is not treated as an automatic one-to-one equivalent.</p><h2>Sources and attribution</h2><p>Claims retain their publisher, date and source type. Government, vendor, academic and other reporting are not blended into a single unattributed conclusion. Source reporting, APT Notes assessment, disputed attribution and unresolved attribution are clearly separated.</p><p class="callout">Inclusion in APT Notes does not independently confirm every public attribution claim associated with an actor.</p><h2>Confidence</h2><dl>{Object.entries(confidenceDefinitions).map(([name,definition])=><><dt><strong>{name.charAt(0).toUpperCase()+name.slice(1)}</strong></dt><dd>{definition}</dd></>)}</dl><p>Confidence is qualitative and never presented as a numeric probability.</p><h2>Machine-readable exports</h2><p>Static JSON exports preserve the public record as rendered at build time. Consumers must retain source references, review dates and confidence. The exports are not an IOC feed and should not be converted into automatic blocking decisions.</p><h2>Updates and corrections</h2><p>Substantive changes are written to the structured update collection. Corrections identify what changed without silently rewriting analytical history.</p><h2>Limitations</h2><p>Public reporting is incomplete, collection access is unequal, and actor boundaries change. A profile is a reviewed representation of available evidence, not a claim of omniscience or a live operational feed.</p><h2>Privacy</h2><p>APT Notes uses no advertising cookies, fingerprinting or external search telemetry. Search is generated locally in the production build. Optional aggregate, cookie-free measurement follows the <a href="https://hecavex.com/en/privacy/">HECAVEX privacy policy</a> and honours Do Not Track.</p></article></BaseLayout>
